## 2. First, Understand the Entire Existing Website

Before changing anything, investigate the existing application thoroughly.

Understand the website as a complete product rather than treating the admin console as an isolated page.

Inspect all relevant parts of the project, including:

- The public-facing website and its design system.
- The existing admin console and all accessible admin pages.
- Existing navigation structures and user journeys.
- Available application features and business workflows.
- Authentication and authorization mechanisms.
- User roles and permission models.
- Database schemas, relationships, and existing data.
- Backend APIs, server actions, and integrations.
- Existing analytics, event tracking, and reporting capabilities.
- Content management functionality.
- Existing settings, configuration screens, and operational tools.
- Notifications, emails, and communication workflows.
- Existing third-party integrations.
- Error handling, loading states, and empty states.
- Existing responsive layouts and mobile behavior.
- Existing reusable components, design tokens, and UI libraries.
- Existing automated tests and deployment configuration.

Inspect the actual implementation wherever possible. Do not rely solely on the appearance of the interface or assumptions about what the application does.

Determine which features are fully functional, partially implemented, visually present but nonfunctional, duplicated, inaccessible, difficult to discover, or completely absent.

Identify dependencies between features before making architectural decisions.

### Important constraints

- Preserve all existing working functionality unless a deliberate replacement is justified.
- Do not discard valuable existing business logic simply to simplify the redesign.
- Do not break public-facing pages, user journeys, APIs, or existing integrations.
- Reuse existing infrastructure when it is appropriate and reliable.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies, duplicate implementations, and architectural complexity.
- Never assume a feature exists just because its UI suggests that it does.
- Never assume a feature is missing until the relevant code, routes, APIs, and data structures have been investigated.
- If something cannot be inspected, clearly identify the limitation rather than inventing findings.

The first deliverable must be a reliable understanding of the current system.

