## 4. Build a Comprehensive Feature Gap Matrix

After completing the audit, compare the existing capabilities against the requirements of a mature, modern administrative platform.

Identify three distinct groups:

### A. What already exists and works

Document what should be retained, including any capabilities that are already excellent.

### B. What exists but should be enhanced

Identify opportunities to improve functionality, usability, performance, security, reporting, discoverability, or visual consistency.

### C. What is missing and should be added

Identify important capabilities that would make the admin console more self-sufficient and eliminate unnecessary dependence on external tools.

Do not add features indiscriminately. Recommend features based on the actual website, its business model, its data, and its operational requirements.

For each proposed feature, explain:

- What problem it solves.
- Who would use it.
- How it would work.
- Why it belongs in the admin console.
- What data or infrastructure it requires.
- Whether it can be implemented using existing infrastructure.
- Its implementation complexity.
- Its priority.
- How its success will be measured.

Distinguish essential capabilities from advanced capabilities and optional capabilities.

If the website's business model is unclear, infer only what the code and existing functionality support. Document uncertainties and avoid building business-specific modules on unsupported assumptions.

