## 10. Implement Real Functionality, Not Just a Visual Prototype

This is one of the most important requirements.

**Every implemented control must perform its advertised action.**

Do not deliver a beautiful interface filled with nonfunctional buttons, placeholder data, fake metrics, static charts, or incomplete workflows.

For every feature:

- Implement the necessary frontend.
- Implement the backend behavior.
- Connect it to the correct data source.
- Add validation and error handling.
- Enforce permissions.
- Provide appropriate loading and success states.
- Handle empty results.
- Handle network failures.
- Test the complete workflow.

For features requiring external services, determine whether an existing integration can be reused or configured.

If a feature depends on missing credentials, infrastructure, or a third-party account, implement the supported parts and clearly document the remaining setup requirements.

Never fabricate credentials, invent API responses, claim an integration is connected when it is not, or represent sample data as live production data.

If mock data is needed during development, isolate it clearly and ensure the production interface cannot silently mistake it for real information.

